[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what was changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mRoot cause:[22m A mismatch in how [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m / [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m are called between the C and Rust code.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The C BLAKE implementation's [38;5;10mblake256_update[0m and [38;5;10mblake512_update[0m functions take their [38;5;10mdatalen[0m parameter in [1mbits[22m, not bytes. Internally they do things like [38;5;10mleft = S->buflen >> 3[0m and [38;5;10mdatalen >= 1024[0m (for 128-byte blocks).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
However, the C code in [38;5;10mhash_blake.c[0m and [38;5;10mPQCgenKAT_sign.c[0m calls these functions with [1mbyte[22m counts:[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10mblakeX_update(&S, sk_prf, SPX_N);      // SPX_N = 32, passed as-is (not *8)
blakeX_update(&S, optrand, SPX_N);
blakeX_update(ctx, tag, sizeof tag - 1); // 23, passed as-is
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust translator noticed the update functions expect bits and "helpfully corrected" all call sites by multiplying by 8:[0m[0m
[1mrust
[0m[38;5;10mblake512_update(&mut s, &sk_prf[..SPX_N], (SPX_N as u64) * 8);  // 256 bits
blake512_update(&mut ctx.state, tag, (tag.len() as u64) * 8);     // 184 bits
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This changed the semantics — the C code hashes fewer bytes per update call than the Rust code did. While the C code's convention looks like a bug, it's the reference implementation that produces the expected KAT vectors, so the Rust translation must match it exactly.[0m[0m
